{
  "schema_version": 2,
  "source": "# Estuary research log\n\nThe sampling boat leaves at 06:20. The team fills sterile bottles at three marked stations. Water sampling tracks conditions across the estuary.\n\nChilling collected water slows biological changes before laboratory analysis. The samples stay in a cooler below four degrees Celsius. The laboratory must receive them within six hours.\n\nThe counting hide faces the mudflat. Bird surveys estimate the number of feeding shorebirds at low tide. Observers count for twenty minutes.\n\nTwo observers carry matching notebooks. They record birds independently and reconcile their totals after the survey. Independent counts help identify errors in the shorebird survey.\n\nRaised boardwalks keep visitors away from sensitive marsh vegetation. The eastern route is 900 meters long. Bicycles are not permitted on the boardwalk.\n\nA warning lamp is fixed to the entrance gate. The boardwalk closes when the predicted tide exceeds the marked safety level. Rangers reopen it only after inspecting the surface.\n",
  "source_name": "wetland.md",
